class:: Tristimulus
summary:: Relative amplitude in three harmonic groups
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Tonal
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/HPCP, Classes/HarmonicPeaks

description::
Shared interpolated spectral peaks are associated with the nearest integer harmonic of the supplied F0. Only the strongest matching peak occupies each harmonic slot. Missing harmonics retain zero slots; an incorrect F0 or tight tolerance changes the result. The formulas are explicit MIR2 conventions in the harmonic-descriptor tradition summarized by Peeters (2004); this is independent code, not a wrapper around another analysis library. Groups are the first harmonic, harmonics 2 through 4, and harmonics 5 upward. Each amplitude sum is divided by total associated amplitude; this is amplitude weighting, not power weighting.



classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain. This analyzer updates when a new frame arrives and holds its output between frames.

argument:: f0
Supplied fundamental in Hz, for example the first output of Pitch2. Nonpositive or invalid values disable harmonic association.

argument:: maxHarmonics
Literal number of harmonic slots from 1 through 64. Harmonic index is stable when partials are missing.

argument:: tolerance
Maximum distance in cents between a peak and its nearest integer multiple of f0, clamped from 0 through 600. Default 50.

argument:: peakThreshold
Minimum local-peak magnitude relative to the largest included spectral peak, from 0 through 1. Up to 128 strongest peaks are retained.

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192, matching the FFT buffer. Frequency resolution and first-result latency depend on this size.

returns::
Three fractions summing to one on nonzero harmonic input.

method:: fromAudio
Convenience constructor: accepts audio and builds Pitch2 plus FFT internally, then calls kr with the estimated F0. The input range defaults to 55 through 1760 Hz, FFT size 4096 and hop fraction 0.25; it accepts the same harmonic controls. Use kr when sharing an existing pitch estimate or FFT chain.

examples::
Tristimulus partitions harmonic partial energy into three psychoacoustic bands: fundamental ($T_1$), low harmonics 2--4 ($T_2$), and high harmonics 5+ ($T_3$). In this example, these three energy proportions continuously morph between three auxiliary synth layers: fundamental depth (sub sine), mid-range warmth (resonant triangle), and upper spectral shimmer (sparkling high partials). An interactive code::MIR2GUI.tristimulus:: plots the coordinates on a barycentric ternary triangle.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("melody.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 3);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic melody fallback
            Saw.ar(Select.kr(LFNoise0.kr(2).range(0, 5), [220, 261.63, 293.66, 329.63, 392, 440])) * 0.2
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var pitch = PitchMPM.kr(source, 80, 1600, 2048, 0.25);
        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(2048), source, 0.25);
        var tri = Tristimulus.kr(chain, pitch[0], maxHarmonics: 16, fftSize: 2048);
        var t1 = tri[0].lag(0.04);
        var t2 = tri[1].lag(0.04);
        var t3 = tri[2].lag(0.04);
        var f0 = pitch[0].clip(80, 1500);

        // Three distinct timbral textures weighted by T1, T2, T3
        var sub = SinOsc.ar(f0 * 0.5, 0, t1 * 0.2);
        var mid = LFTri.ar(f0, 0, t2 * 0.15);
        var high = BPF.ar(Saw.ar(f0 * 3), f0 * 4, 0.2) * (t3 * 0.25);
        var synthLayer = (sub + mid + high) * pitch[1].lag(0.05);
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + synthLayer;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, tri) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.tristimulus("Harmonic Tristimulus", ~mir2Bus);
});
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

